WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.000
Good morning.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.200
My name is Ben Busby, and I'm the Genomics Outreach Coordinator for NCBI.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:15.800
Thank you for coming to our short, informal webinar series, The NCBI Minute.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:24.980
Our NCBI Minute installment this week is going to be on BioProject, a fast way to get genomic data from NCBI.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:33.000
A video recording of this webinar will be available on our YouTube channel very soon,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.000
and please remember to subscribe to our YouTube channel.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:43.460
Also, all of the questions that you guys ask will be available in this document after the webinar.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:51.180
I'd like to tell you, in three minutes or less, how to get the genomic data you want from an NCBI.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:58.000
If you were to search for a term like "mouse" or "tuberculosis" in the traditional Entrez search,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.820
you would get somewhere on the order of eight million hits for mouse, anyway.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:09.700
The advantage of using the BioProject database is that there is a faceted search bar on the left.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:16.440
For example, if I wanted RNA-seq data, I could facet by Transcriptome and SRA.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:21.240
SRA is the Sequence Read Archive where we keep our next generation sequencing data.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:27.160
And then that would specifically give me RNA-seq datasets for mouse.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:34.120
Similarly, if I wanted epigenomics data, I could facet by Epigenomics.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:41.880
If I click on one example of this -- I believe it's the fourth or fifth link down -- I get one particular project.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:53.000
So here, I get an umbrella project of 224 SRA experiments linked to a PubMed abstract and also GEO DataSets,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:58.480
which are pre-analyzed data analyzed by the investigators that submitted this data.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:08.000
Some of the features of this are being able to look at the abstract that they've submitted to BioProject.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Now, what you'll notice is this is a much more data-centered abstract than the corresponding PubMed abstract,

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.160
which really talks about the entire study and the paper that they have published.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.860
If we go back to BioProject, we can also click on the link for SRA experiments.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.640
Note that this is an active link.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.020
We can go there; and then once we get to SRA,

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:37.720
we can send those to something called the Run Selector from the Send To button.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:44.700
And then we're able to filter farther via this SOLAR-based filtering here on the left.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:54.160
For example, if I wanted to filter by assembly name, I could select Assembly Name here and select GRCh37.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:03:02.820
Also, if I go to the SRA data and go to the Send To button, I can select some of these studies

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:07.360
and send them to my standard BLAST interface.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:18.680
In that way, I can BLAST any particular gene or nucleotide sequence that I'm interested in into these SRA runs.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:23.680
And, as we can see here, I get a standard BLAST output.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.860
Soon, we will also have this output available in SAM format.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:33.260
Finally, I'd like to talk about reporting.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:43.420
As many of you know, the NIH genomic data sharing standards will be in effect as of September 2015.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:51.000
PIs will have to document sharing of data with public repositories, such as NCBI.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:58.580
BioProject accession numbers make it very easy for PIs to document their genomic data sharing projects

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:02.760
in the NIH grant project review system.

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:08.720
Once again, thank you very much for attending our NCBI Minute.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:10.480
My name is Ben Busby.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:14.380
Please subscribe to our YouTube channel. And have a great day.

